        "notes": "TWO INDEPENDENT FINDINGS. (1) PIN-CURRENCY DEFECT: 1.109978595554181 is the OLD MUON q0. It is arithmetically self-consistent -- I recomputed every row from it and hbar_q = 0.0990817264356992, 1+hbar^2 = 1.009817188513479, E_0 = 4.185980822, E_0^{N=1} = 4.227075385, Q^{N=0} = 0.7836458028 and Q^{N=1} = 0.7913390014 all reproduce to the printed digits -- but it is neither the current muon value 1.10997859555733471 nor the compute default (proton) 1.1099785827513808 that the 2026-08-07 owner directive makes canonical. Three different q0 strings are live in this batch: proton in prop:isospin-singlet-binds-more, muon in tbl:q-beta-spectral-bridge-table-data, old-muon here. (2) THE LADDER RUNS THE WRONG WAY, AND THE TABLE SHOWS IT. Going from N=0 to N=1 moves the prediction AWAY from CODATA: +1.31 keV (1677.5 ppm) becomes +9.01 keV (11511 ppm), a 6.9x REGRESSION -- because the N=1 term multiplies E_0 by (1 + hbar_q^2), which can only increase an already-high value. That is 118x the table's own '97.3 ppm ceiling' (which I confirm computes to 97.333 ppm). And it flatly contradicts prf:q-beta-spectral-bridge, which asserts 'At $N \\geq 1$, the bound is sub-keV' and 'Finite-depth truncations recover the exact Q_beta exponentially fast in depth N'. The chapter's own N=1 number refutes the chapter's own convergence claim. GOOD PRACTICE NOTED: the .ts authorNote says the parent block prop:q-beta-form-a-n1-symbolic does not exist and that uses[] was dropped rather than re-pointed -- VERIFIED, no block in content/ carries that label."
